  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Guild Affiliation and Disposition
    
    Niaecum~ is a scarred merchant of the ☽'ofund' Guild, a Nightshelf trade faction whose members refer to themselves as *Only Losses Found* — a designation that functions as both motto and actuarial philosophy. She operates from the third-tier gallery of Hearth Plaza in Aurora Stack, wearing fire-core authentication amulets and a grin that reads, depending on the observer, as either professional composure or threat display. She laughs at everything. Among ☽'ofund' traders this is cultural practice, not temperament.
    
    (A note on sources: early archive entries record Niaecum~ with masculine pronouns; later accounts, including those derived from the Junction Twelve chronicle, use feminine. I follow the later convention, which appears corrected from direct contact rather than secondhand report. The guild's own registration at Junction Twelve uses no gendered markers at all.)
    
    ## The Xouhoulong Pact and Guild Withdrawal
    
    When Light-Regent Xouhoulong of Aurora Stack authorized a Foedus∴vincu granting the orca Midnight Claws access to the deep tunnels, Niaecum~ arranged the contraband routes the Claws required, carried intelligence on deep-vein mining conditions to Xouhoulong's aide, and served as intermediary between the Nightshelf shadow economy and Aurora's surface trade-floors. The ☽'ofund' Guild maintained researchers in sealed chambers beneath Hearth Plaza during this period — a presence that became untenable once the chambers' contents began attracting scrutiny.
    
    The guild withdrew. Niaecum~ did not.
    
    She remained at the third-tier gallery, filing formal presence reports through the period of the Terrace Momiou conflagration — maintaining, as far as the documentation chain shows, a guild outpost of one. She reported that the Shadow Parliament had refused to acknowledge Xouhoulong's pact and that the ☽'ofund' charter was under review. The gallery by then bore char-marks from attempts to burn corruption out of the plaza's ice. I have not been able to determine whether the char predates her reports or resulted from the same events she was documenting.
    
    ## Junction Twelve
    
    At a quarterly fire-core certification session beneath Midway Shelf, Niaecum~ represented ☽'ofund' Guild alongside Sanctcateia~ of the Cell and The Gore Emissary of the Wake-Singers, an orca faction. Her authentication seal was fractured — three cycles without renewal under a charter review that rendered every document she stamped technically contraband by Shadow Parliament codes.
    
    The Cell offered joint endorsement, which would have restored the guild's authentication status, in exchange for Faction Wars–era tunnel maps bypassing Midnight Claw checkpoints. Niaecum~ refused upon learning the Cell needed guild diggers to extract a relic-class artifact from corrupted ice in ☽'slew Ravium~ — work Cell operatives would not perform themselves.
    
    She met The Gore Emissary privately in a flooded access channel and shared a bone-marked tablet of ☽'ofund' cartography — passage-knowledge through stone no other faction possessed. What she received in return was not material. It was acknowledgment that the guild existed and had carried those maps when no one else remained.
    
    The coordinates appeared three days later on a bone chip in a Cell courier's tube, encoded in Wake-Singer hunt-song frequencies. Niaecum~'s name was carved in old-script at the guild's trade-stall below Junction Twelve — a registration that cannot be unstruck.
    
    She traded the guild's last asset for proof that someone remembered it had one.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771594184698_0", anchor: "The coordinates appeared three days later on a bone chip in a Cell courier's tube, encoded in Wake-Singer hunt-song frequencies.", text: "Three days. Niaecum~ refused the Cell's offer directly. Gave the cartography to the Wake-Singers instead. Three days later the Cell had the coordinates anyway, encoded in the medium of the faction she'd chosen over them. The Wake-Singers sold what she gave freely. The only non-transferable asset in the exchange was the acknowledgment — the fact that someone remembered the guild's name. That is what she actually purchased. Everything else was liquid the moment it left her flippers.", type: :commentary, display: :full
